# Agent envs
PORT=8080
AWS_REGION=us-east-1
BEDROCK_MODEL_ID=us.anthropic.claude-sonnet-5

# Ceiling on a single /invocations body, in characters. The BFF's own MAX_MESSAGE_LENGTH already
# bounds what reaches here and the BFF is the runtime's only caller, so this is the ceiling that
# holds if anything ever invokes the runtime directly. Default 20000.
# MAX_BODY_LENGTH=20000

# ── What one turn may cost ────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# The three below bound the agent LOOP, and they are the only thing that does. Everything else in
# this template bounds a different axis: the BFF's per-caller quota bounds how OFTEN the agent is
# called, MAX_BODY_LENGTH bounds how LARGE one request is. Neither says anything about how many model
# calls one request may cause — and Strands treats an omitted cap as "no limit on that dimension", so
# an unset ceiling here is an unbounded one.
#
# Why this matters more than it looks: a turn that runs away produces no error. The chat Lambda's 60s
# timeout ends the RELAY, not the loop, so the container keeps calling Bedrock for an answer nobody is
# reading and the tokens are still billed. (The runtime now also cancels the loop when the caller
# disconnects, which covers the common case; these are what cover a turn that is genuinely looping.)
#
# A cap firing is logged as `turn.limited` with the correlation id, because a capped turn returns 200
# with a short answer — indistinguishable, otherwise, from a model that had nothing more to say.

# Model calls per turn, where a turn is one model call plus any tool execution after it. The
# predictable bound and the one to tune first: an ordinary exchange needs one to three. Default 10.
# AGENT_MAX_TURNS=10

# Cumulative input + output tokens across every model call in one turn — the closest thing to "what
# this request will be billed". A backstop rather than the primary control, and deliberately generous:
# every call re-sends the context it was given, so the counter compounds, and with MEMORY_MAX_MESSAGES
# at 40 a long conversation can legitimately reach six figures. Set it too tight and it truncates real
# answers. A soft cap — the loop stops at the first turn boundary past the budget. Default 400000.
# AGENT_MAX_TOTAL_TOKENS=400000

# The hard ceiling on a SINGLE model response, passed to the model rather than to the loop, so it is
# the one cap that cannot be overshot. Lowering it does not stop a runaway — it truncates a legitimate
# reply mid-sentence. Default 8192.
# AGENT_MAX_OUTPUT_TOKENS=8192

# ── Conversation memory ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# The AgentCore Memory resource conversations are recorded in, set by
# infra/src/stacks/agent-stack.ts. Left unset here on purpose: `npm run dev` then answers every turn
# without history and records nothing, which keeps local development free of a managed resource.
# Encryption and retention are properties of that resource, not of this process.
# AGENTCORE_MEMORY_ID=my_project_memory-XXXXXXXXXX

# How many past messages are replayed into a new turn. Each turn re-sends the context it is given,
# so an uncapped history makes a long conversation cost more with every message. Default 40.
# MEMORY_MAX_MESSAGES=40

# ── Content guardrail ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# A Bedrock guardrail id and version. Both must be set for the guardrail to apply — a half-configured
# pair is treated as absent rather than as an error, so a demo without one still runs. The deployment
# gate (infra/src/config.ts) requires them under DEPLOY_PROFILE=pilot and prod, where "no content
# filter" stops being an acceptable answer.
# BEDROCK_GUARDRAIL_ID=abcd1234efgh
# BEDROCK_GUARDRAIL_VERSION=1

# ── Telemetry ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# The Strands Agent emits spans and token/tool metrics on its own, but they reach a no-op provider
# until one is registered. THIS is the switch — AgentCore's own variable name, passed to the container
# unchanged by infra/src/stacks/agent-stack.ts, so what turns telemetry on in the stack and what turns
# it on in the runtime cannot drift apart.
#
# Note it is NOT `OTEL_EXPORTER_OTLP_ENDPOINT`. That gate encoded a model AWS has retired: its
# documentation now states the ADOT Collector is not supported for agent observability, and the
# supported path is a direct, SigV4-signed export to regional CloudWatch endpoints (src/otlp-sigv4.ts).
# Nothing here reads a collector address.
# AGENT_OBSERVABILITY_ENABLED=true

# Names this runtime in every span and metric, and is the `ServiceName` dimension the dashboard reads.
# Defaults to `agent`.
# OTEL_SERVICE_NAME=my-project-agent

# The rest of the telemetry configuration is DERIVED BY THE STACK, not set by hand — the log group the
# spans are directed to and the log group carrying the retention, the CMK and the data protection
# policy have to be the same log group by construction. Listed here only so a local run can point them
# somewhere, and so the deployed values are recognizable in the console:
#
#   AGENT_METRICS_LOG_GROUP           where EMF metric records are written
#   AGENT_METRICS_NAMESPACE           the CloudWatch namespace derived from them
#   OTEL_EXPORTER_OTLP_TRACES_HEADERS x-aws-log-group / x-aws-log-stream — without these the endpoint
#                                     files spans under the account-shared `aws/spans`, where none of
#                                     the deployment's controls reach them
#   OTEL_RESOURCE_ATTRIBUTES          service.name plus aws.log.group.names, which is what offers a
#                                     span's surrounding log lines in the console
#
# Unset, `AGENT_METRICS_LOG_GROUP` makes the metric exporter a no-op rather than an error, so
# `AGENT_OBSERVABILITY_ENABLED=true` alone gives you spans locally without a log group to write to.

# Invoke script envs (npm run invoke:bedrock)
# AWS_REGION is already defined above, but you can override it here if needed.
# The script signs with SigV4 from your ambient AWS profile — the only way into the runtime, which
# carries no JWT authorizer. Your profile needs bedrock-agentcore:InvokeAgentRuntime on this ARN.
AGENT_RUNTIME_ARN=arn:aws:bedrock-agentcore:us-east-1:YOUR_ACCOUNT_ID:runtime/my-agent-service-XXXXXXXXXX
